title: 'Spec v3 reviewed: local runner leaves the kernel, hardware keys, one Move 1'
summary: 'Aegis (research 22) and Arky (23) reviewed spec v3 and independently converged on the same top findings: the local runner sits inside the key-holding kernel (pi-ai bundles cloud SDKs, verified); the root key is not truly presence-bound; mods can exceed their declared effects. Founder decisions follow; spec v3.1 next.'
symbols_touched:
  - '#paradigm-harness'
decisions:
  - id: P8-D36
    decision: Keep one Move 1 (no M1a/M1b split), despite both Arky and Fable recommending the split
    rationale: Founder's call; scope unchanged.
  - id: P8-D37
    decision: Drop the Claude-Code-to-local-endpoint probe; local models run only through Gnomon's own local runner (gnomon-local child process); noted for 8.1
    rationale: Law 7 refuses the endpoint override by construction; the probe would expose the OAuth token; CC's prompt overflows small local contexts.
  - id: P8-D38
    decision: 'Approvals use a hardware-backed key: one Touch ID / Windows Hello unlock when the TUI attaches, held in memory, dropped after 15 min idle; always a fresh prompt for gate-class, install, KNOT resolve, verdict, cloud fallback and budget resume; CLI approve without the TUI prompts every time. Supersedes the key-file part of P8-D12.'
    rationale: Removes the on-disk approval key that sandbox escapes could read, without habituating the founder to blind taps.
  - id: P8-D39
    decision: Root key becomes a non-exportable hardware key (Secure Enclave P-256 on macOS, TPM / Windows Hello P-256 on Windows) via small signed native helpers; the Windows helper also creates the named pipe with a DACL and first-instance; agent keys stay Ed25519
    rationale: 'Aegis C2 and Arky: keystore items are readable by same-user processes and presence checks need signed native code.'
